                <h1>Is MyProtein Impact Whey Safe? Lead Testing &amp; the Prop 65 Case Nobody Mentions</h1>

                <div class="article-meta">
                    <span>📅 Published: July 10, 2026</span>
                    <span>⏱️ 9 min read</span>
                    <span>📊 Labdoor COA · Informed Choice · Prop 65 settlement record</span>
                </div>

                <!-- DIRECT ANSWER -->
                <div class="direct-answer-box">
                    <h2 style="font-size: 20px;">✅ Direct Answer: Is MyProtein Impact Whey Safe?</h2>
                    <p style="margin-bottom: 0; font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.6;">
                        <strong>Yes, on the current evidence — with a history worth knowing.</strong> Independent Labdoor testing of <strong>Impact Whey Isolate</strong> found <strong>arsenic, cadmium and mercury undetected</strong> and <strong>lead below the limit of quantitation</strong> (a passing result). It's <strong>Informed Choice</strong> and <strong>Informed Protein</strong> certified. But MyProtein's parent company <strong>settled a California Prop 65 lead/cadmium case for $198,000 in 2020</strong>. We rate it <strong>Verified Safe on current testing</strong> — and we think you should know both halves.
                        <a href="#prop65">Jump to the Prop 65 history →</a>
                    </p>
                </div>

                <p style="font-size: 19px; font-weight: 600; line-height: 1.6; color: #1a1a18; background: #f0f7f3; padding: 24px; border-left: 5px solid #2d6a4f; border-radius: 8px;">
                    Most reviews of MyProtein cite its excellent lab scores. Almost none mention that the company paid nearly $200,000 to settle a California lead case. Both facts are true, and you deserve both.
                </p>

                <!-- THE NUMBERS -->
                <section id="the-numbers">
                    <h2>The Independent Test Results</h2>

                    <p>Labdoor — which buys products off the shelf, tests them, and publishes the full Certificate of Analysis — tested MyProtein Impact Whey Isolate:</p>

                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Heavy Metal</th>
                                <th>Result</th>
                                <th>USP Daily Limit</th>
                                <th>Status</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr class="safe-row">
                                <td><strong>Lead</strong></td>
                                <td><strong>Below LOQ*</strong></td>
                                <td>5 µg/serving</td>
                                <td>✅ PASS</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr class="safe-row">
                                <td><strong>Arsenic</strong></td>
                                <td><strong>Undetected</strong></td>
                                <td>15 µg/serving</td>
                                <td>✅ PASS</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr class="safe-row">
                                <td><strong>Cadmium</strong></td>
                                <td><strong>Undetected</strong></td>
                                <td>5 µg/serving</td>
                                <td>✅ PASS</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr class="safe-row">
                                <td><strong>Mercury</strong></td>
                                <td><strong>Undetected</strong></td>
                                <td>15 µg/serving</td>
                                <td>✅ PASS</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <p style="font-size:14px;color:#666;">*LOQ = Limit of Quantitation. Lot U41582255, sampled November–December 2024, 1 scoop (31.5g). Accuracy also passed: 25g protein claimed, 25.3g found.</p>

                    <div class="highlight-box yellow">
                        <h4>⚠️ "Below LOQ" and "Undetected" Are Not the Same Thing</h4>
                        <p>This is a distinction most review sites flatten, and it matters.</p>
                        <ul>
                            <li><strong>Undetected</strong> — the instrument found <em>nothing</em>. This is what the same lab reported for lead in <a href="now-sports-whey-protein-lead-testing-safety-2026.html">NOW Sports Whey Isolate</a>.</li>
                            <li><strong>Below LOQ</strong> — something may be there, but it's below the level the lab can reliably <em>quantify</em>. It's a pass, and it's a good result. It is <em>not</em> quite as clean as "undetected."</li>
                        </ul>
                        <p style="margin-bottom:0;">Practically? Both are far below the 5 µg USP limit, and both are fine for daily use. But if you're comparing two proteins and one says "undetected" while the other says "below LOQ," you now know which is the stronger result — and that nobody else will tell you.</p>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <!-- CERTIFICATION -->
                <section id="certification">
                    <h2>The Certifications: Informed Choice + Informed Protein</h2>

                    <p>MyProtein carries two meaningful certifications on Impact Whey Isolate:</p>

                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Certification</th>
                                <th>What It Verifies</th>
                                <th>Heavy Metals?</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr class="safe-row">
                                <td><strong>Informed Choice</strong></td>
                                <td>Regular random testing for banned substances and contaminants; global sports-nutrition QC standard</td>
                                <td>⚠️ Contaminants — banned substances are the focus</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr class="safe-row">
                                <td><strong>Informed Protein</strong></td>
                                <td>Verifies label protein accuracy — guards against amino-acid/nitrogen "spiking"</td>
                                <td>❌ No — this is about label honesty</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>

                    <div class="callout-box">
                        <p><strong>Read the certifications for what they are.</strong> Informed Protein is a genuinely good thing — protein spiking is a real industry fraud — but it tells you <em>nothing</em> about lead. Informed Choice is a real contaminant program, but banned substances are its headline. <strong>The Labdoor COA is what actually speaks to heavy metals here</strong>, which is why we lead with it rather than the logos.</p>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <!-- POLL -->
                <div class="poll-container">
                    <h3>⚡ Quick Check: Is YOUR Protein Safe?</h3>
                    <p>Tell us what you're using — we'll show you the safety data in 5 seconds:</p>
                    <form name="proteinPoll" id="proteinPoll">
                        <label for="brandInput" style="font-weight: 600; display: block; margin-bottom: 8px;">What protein do you currently use?</label>
                        <input type="text" id="brandInput" name="brand" placeholder="e.g., MyProtein, NOW Sports, Orgain, ON Gold Standard..."
