                        <label style="display: block; margin-bottom: 20px;"><input type="radio" name="type" value="Switching" style="margin-right: 8px;"> I'm switching to something safer</label>
                        <button type="button" onclick="submitPollAndRedirect()" class="poll-button">Check Safety Status →</button>
                    </form>
                    <div id="pollResult" style="display: none; margin-top: 15px;"></div>
                </div>
                <script src="/includes/protein-poll.js"></script>

                <!-- PROP 65 -->
                <section id="prop65">
                    <h2>The $198,000 Prop 65 Settlement Nobody Mentions</h2>

                    <p>Here's the part that's missing from every "best protein powder" list that features MyProtein.</p>

                    <div class="highlight-box red">
                        <h4>⚖️ The Record</h4>
                        <ul>
                            <li><strong>May &amp; June 2019</strong> — the <strong>Environmental Research Center</strong> filed California Proposition 65 Notices of Violation alleging that MyProtein products contained <strong>lead and/or cadmium</strong> at levels requiring a Prop 65 warning.</li>
                            <li><strong>September 2019</strong> — ERC filed a civil complaint against <strong>The Hut Group Limited, dba MyProtein</strong>.</li>
                            <li><strong>June 2020</strong> — settlement reached.</li>
                            <li><strong>September 8, 2020</strong> — judgment entered. The Hut Group paid <strong>$198,000 total</strong> in civil penalties, settlement payments, attorney's fees and costs, and agreed to corrective action.</li>
                        </ul>
                        <p style="margin-bottom:0;">Prop 65 is a <em>warning-label</em> law — the allegation was failure to warn about exposure levels, not that the product was acutely poisonous. But this was a real case with a real payment and a real corrective-action agreement.</p>
                    </div>

                    <h3>So How Much Should That Worry You?</h3>

                    <p>Honestly? <strong>Less than you'd think — because of what came after.</strong> Here's the timeline that matters:</p>

                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>When</th>
                                <th>What Happened</th>
                                <th>What It Tells Us</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr class="danger-row">
                                <td><strong>2019–2020</strong></td>
                                <td>Prop 65 lead/cadmium case → $198,000 settlement + corrective action</td>
                                <td>There was a real problem</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr class="safe-row">
                                <td><strong>Nov–Dec 2024</strong></td>
                                <td>Labdoor COA: 3 metals undetected, lead below LOQ, purity PASS</td>
                                <td>Independent testing now looks clean</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr class="safe-row">
                                <td><strong>Current</strong></td>
                                <td>Informed Choice + Informed Protein certified</td>
                                <td>Ongoing third-party surveillance</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>

                    <div class="highlight-box green">
                        <h4>✅ Our Read: A Company That Was Caught, and Then Fixed It</h4>
                        <p style="margin-bottom:0;">The 2019 case is <strong>six years old</strong>. The corrective action was agreed. And the most recent <em>independent</em> evidence — a 2024 Labdoor COA on an off-the-shelf tub — passes cleanly. That's a substantially better story than a brand with <em>no</em> testing history at all. We'd rather buy from a company that got caught and cleaned up than one nobody has ever checked. But you should know the history, and now you do.</p>
                    </div>

                    <div class="callout-box">
                        <p><strong>One thing we won't do:</strong> pretend the 2019 case tells you what's in the tub today, or pretend it never happened. Old test results don't describe current products — that cuts <em>both</em> ways. The 2024 COA is the current evidence, and it's good.</p>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <!-- PRODUCT LINES -->
                <section id="product-lines">
                    <h2>Impact Whey vs. Impact Whey Isolate — Not the Same Product</h2>

                    <div class="callout-box">
                        <p><strong>Read your tub carefully.</strong> MyProtein sells <strong>Impact Whey Protein</strong> (a concentrate) and <strong>Impact Whey Isolate</strong> (~90% protein, more heavily filtered). <strong>They are different products with different test results.</strong> The Labdoor COA cited on this page is for the <strong>Isolate</strong>, lot U41582255.</p>
                        <p style="margin-bottom:0;">MyProtein also sells a large catalog including <strong>plant-based (pea/rice) proteins</strong>, blends, and clear whey. Plant proteins carry substantially higher category risk — Consumer Reports found 14 of 15 exceeded safe lead limits. <strong>We never transfer a result from one product line to another</strong>, and neither should you.</p>
                    </div>

                    <p>On the category question: Impact Whey Isolate is <strong>whey</strong>, which is the right place to be. Consumer Reports found whey averaged roughly <strong>9× less lead than plant proteins</strong>, and 6 of the 7 safest products tested were whey-based.</p>
                </section>

                <!-- COMPARISON -->
                <section id="comparison">
