                <!-- FAQ -->
                <section id="faq">
                    <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
                    <div class="faq-premier">
                        <div class="faq-item-premier">
                            <h4>Is MyProtein Impact Whey safe?</h4>
                            <p><strong>Yes, on current independent testing.</strong> Labdoor's 2024 COA on Impact Whey Isolate found arsenic, cadmium and mercury undetected and lead below the limit of quantitation, passing purity and accuracy. It's Informed Choice and Informed Protein certified. The caveat is historical: MyProtein's parent settled a Prop 65 lead/cadmium case for $198,000 in 2020.</p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="faq-item-premier">
                            <h4>Does MyProtein Impact Whey have lead?</h4>
                            <p><strong>Lead was "below LOQ" — below the limit of quantitation.</strong> That means any lead present was too low for the lab to reliably measure, against a 5 µg/serving USP limit. It's a passing result. It's a step below "undetected" (which is what the same lab found for <a href="now-sports-whey-protein-lead-testing-safety-2026.html">NOW Sports</a>), but both are far under the limit and fine for daily use.</p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="faq-item-premier">
                            <h4>Did MyProtein get sued over lead?</h4>
                            <p><strong>Yes — and it settled.</strong> The Environmental Research Center filed California Prop 65 notices in 2019 alleging lead and/or cadmium in MyProtein products above warning thresholds. The Hut Group Limited (dba MyProtein) settled in June 2020, with judgment entered September 8, 2020, paying <strong>$198,000</strong> total plus corrective action. That's six years ago, and the independent 2024 lab testing since then passes cleanly.</p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="faq-item-premier">
                            <h4>Should the 2020 settlement stop me buying MyProtein?</h4>
                            <p><strong>Not on its own.</strong> Old results don't describe current tubs — a principle we apply in both directions. The most recent <em>independent</em> evidence (Labdoor, late 2024, bought off the shelf) is good. A brand that was caught, paid, took corrective action, and now passes independent testing is arguably a safer bet than a brand nobody has ever tested. Know the history; weigh the current data.</p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="faq-item-premier">
                            <h4>Is Impact Whey the same as Impact Whey Isolate?</h4>
                            <p><strong>No.</strong> Impact Whey Protein is a <strong>concentrate</strong>; Impact Whey Isolate is a more filtered <strong>isolate</strong> (~90% protein). Different products, different test results. The COA on this page covers the <strong>Isolate</strong>. Never assume one product's results apply to another — that's how misinformation spreads in this category.</p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="faq-item-premier">
                            <h4>What about MyProtein's plant-based proteins?</h4>
                            <p><strong>Different category, higher risk, not covered here.</strong> MyProtein sells pea/rice plant proteins. Consumer Reports found 14 of 15 plant proteins exceeded safe lead limits. Nothing on this page transfers to those lines. For a verified plant option, see <a href="truvani-protein-lead-testing-results-2026.html">Truvani</a> (passed CR January 2026).</p>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <!-- BOTTOM LINE -->
                <section id="bottom-line">
                    <h2>The Bottom Line on MyProtein</h2>
                    <blockquote>
                        <strong>MyProtein Impact Whey Isolate passes on the evidence that exists today.</strong> Independent Labdoor testing found three heavy metals undetected and lead below quantifiable limits, with Informed Choice and Informed Protein certification on top. The part other sites leave out is that MyProtein's parent paid <strong>$198,000 in 2020</strong> to settle a California Prop 65 lead and cadmium case. We think that's context you're entitled to — and we also think it's largely <em>historical</em>, because the current independent testing is clean. If you want the strictly cleaner lead result at a similar price, <a href="now-sports-whey-protein-lead-testing-safety-2026.html">NOW Sports</a> came back "undetected" rather than "below LOQ." If you want the cheapest verified option, ON Gold Standard is still $0.75.
                    </blockquote>

